<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-s03e11" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Episode 11: Calculations with Mixed Numbers</title>
    <assemblage>
        <title>Episode 11 Learning Goals</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Perform calculations involving mixed numbers.
                </p>
            </li>
          </ul>  
        </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
        <alert>How to Perform Calculations Involving Mixed NUmbers</alert>
        </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Convert the mixed numbers to improper fractions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Perform the calculations on those fractions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Convert the answer back to a mixed number.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
      </p>  
      <example>
        <p>
            Perform the calculation:
            <md>
                2\frac{1}{4}+3\frac{1}{4}
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Solution: We start by converting both mixed numbers to improper fractions:
            <md>
                \frac{9}{4}+\frac{13}{4}
            </md>
            We are adding two fractions that have the same denominator. So, we add the two numerators:
            <md>
                \frac{22}{4}
            </md>
            We notice we can simplify this fraction, because both 22 and 4 can be divided by 2:
            <md>
                \frac{22\div 2}{4\div 2} = \frac{11}{2}
            </md>
            Then we convert 11/2 back to a mixed number. The number 2 goes into the number 11 a total of 5 times, with 1 remaining. This gives us the answer:
            <md>
                5\frac{1}{2}
            </md>
            
        </p>
      </example>
      <example>
        <p>
            Perform the calculation:
            <md>
                1\frac{2}{7}\times 3\frac{2}{5}
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Solution: We start by converting both mixed numbers to improper fractions:
            <md>
                \frac{9}{7}\times \frac{17}{5}
            </md>
            Since this is a multiplication problem, we check to see if we can cross-cancel, but we cannot. So, we multiply straight across:
            <md>
                \frac{9\times 17}{7\times 5} = \frac{153}{35}
            </md>
            Now, we convert back to a mixed number. The number 35 goes into 153 a total of 4 times, with 13 left over, so we have:
            <md>
                4\frac{13}{35}
            </md>
            
        </p>
      </example>
      <worksheet xml:id="ws-s03e11">
        <title>Episode 11 Practice Problems</title>
        <p>
            Perform the following calculations:
        </p>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>2\frac{1}{3}+1\frac{3}{4}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>6\frac{7}{8}+2\frac{1}{4}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>9\frac{1}{2}-4\frac{3}{4}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>5\frac{2}{5}+3\frac{1}{10}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>4\frac{5}{6}-2\frac{1}{3}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>7\frac{2}{5}-3\frac{7}{10}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>1\frac{2}{3}\times 2\frac{1}{4}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>4\frac{3}{5}\times 2\frac{2}{3}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>5\frac{3}{4}\div 2\frac{1}{3}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>3\frac{5}{6}\times 1\frac{1}{2}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>2\frac{1}{2}\div 1\frac{1}{4}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.8in">
            <p>
                <m>6\frac{2}{7}\div 1\frac{4}{5}</m>
            </p>    
        </exercise>
      </worksheet>
</section>